t_limit.md prompts automatically injected at the end of a turn.\",\n    75→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    76→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    77→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    78→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-30\",\n    79→    \"topics\": [\"coding-agents\", \"openai\", \"agentic-engineering\", \"codex-cli\", \"system-prompts\"],\n    80→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    81→  },\n    82→  {\n    83→    \"title\": \"UK AI Security Institute evaluation of GPT-5.5 cyber capabilities\",\n    84→    \"description\": \"The UK's AI Security Institute evaluated GPT-5.5 for finding security vulnerabilities and found it to be comparable to Claude Mythos, but unlike Mythos it's generally available right now. This provides important benchmarks for AI security capabilities.\",\n    85→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    86→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    87→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    88→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-30\",\n    89→    \"topics\": [\"ai-security-research\", \"gpt\", \"openai\", \"anthropic\", \"claude\"],\n    90→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    91→  },\n    92→  {\n    93→    \"title\": \"Andrew Kelley on detecting LLM-generated code: 'digital smell'\",\n    94→    \"description\": \"Andrew Kelley, creator of Zig, explains that it's possible to detect who is using LLMs because mistakes humans make are fundamentally different than LLM hallucinations. Furthermore, people from the world of agentic coding have a certain 'digital smell' that is obvious to those who abstain - like when a smoker walks into a room. 'I'm not telling you not to smoke, but I am telling you not to smoke in my house.'\",\n    95→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (quoting Andrew Kelley)\",\n    96→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    97→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    98→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-30\",\n    99→    \"topics\": [\"llms\", \"zig\", \"ai-ethics\", \"generative-ai\"],\n   100→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   101→  },\n   102→  {\n   103→    \"title\": \"We need RSS for sharing abundant vibe-coded apps\",\n   104→    \"description\": \"Matt Webb argues that when vibe-coding accelerates app development, apps become more personal, more situated, and more frequent. Shipping a tool or micro-app is less like launching a website and more like posting on a blog. This inspired Simon to add an Atom feed to his /elsewhere/tools/ page.\",\n   105→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (quoting Matt Webb)\",\n   106→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   107→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n   108→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-30\",\n   109→    \"topics\": [\"rss\", \"atom\", \"vibe-coding\", \"ai\", \"app-distribution\"],\n   110→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   111→  },\n   112→  {\n   113→    \"title\": \"Zig's strict anti-LLM policy and the 'Contributor Poker' philosophy\",\n   114→    \"description\": \"Zig has one of the most stringent anti-LLM policies of any major open source project. Loris Cro, VP of Community at Zig Software Foundation, explains the rationale: Zig values contributors over their contributions. Each contributor represents an investment by the Zig core team - the primary goal is to help grow new contributors who can become trusted and prolific over time. LLM assistance breaks this completely. This relates to the idea that if a PR was mostly written by an LLM, why should a maintainer spend time reviewing it versus firing up their own LLM?\",\n   115→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   116→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   117→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n   118→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-30\",\n   119→    \"topics\": [\"zig\", \"ai\", \"llms\", \"ai-ethics\", \"open-source\", \"ai-assisted-programming\", \"anthropic\", \"bun\", \"javascript\"],\n   120→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   121→  },\n   122→  {\n   123→    \"title\": \"microsoft/VibeVoice - Whisper-style audio model with speaker diarization\",\n   124→    \"description\": \"Microsoft's VibeVoice is a Whisper-style audio model for speech-to-text, MIT licensed and with speaker diarization built into the model. Simon tested it on a Mac using mlx-audio and the 4-bit MLX conversion. The model successfully transcribed an hour-long podcast in 8 minutes 45 seconds on a 128GB M5 Max MacBook Pro, using around 30GB of RAM at peak. The model can only handle up to an hour of audio at a time.\",\n   125→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   126→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   127→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n   128→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-27\",\n   129→    \"topics\": [\"microsoft\", \"python\", \"speech-to-text\", \"mlx\", \"uv\", \"datasette-lite\"],\n   130→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   131→  }\n   132→]\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
